WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:09.320
Here's a ten-ohm resistor with two volts across it. Now we make it four volts. Does twice the voltage always mean twice the current?

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.880
Predict the current at four volts. Then decide whether always is safe.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:31.600
At two volts the current is zero point two amperes. At four volts it is zero point four amperes. Twice the voltage, twice the current, at constant temperature.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:44.440
Voltage over current: two over zero point two is ten ohms. Four over zero point four is also ten ohms. Voltage equals current multiplied by resistance.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:53.960
Always is not safe. A filament lamp heats up, its resistance rises, so doubling the voltage does not double the current.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:07.320
At constant temperature, an ohmic conductor's current is proportional to voltage. Voltage equals current multiplied by resistance. A filament lamp breaks the rule: its resistance rises.

